<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slovenia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Slovenia is a country in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, the Pannonian plain and the Adriatic Sea, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a>. Its lands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-styria">Lower Styria</a> and part of the Littoral, were ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> for centuries before joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a> in 1918 and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. It declared independence in 1991 and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2004.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slovenia.jpg" alt="Slovenia" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Marko Pogačnik Vector: Achim1999 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Slovenia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Slovenia covers 20,271 km2 and is mostly mountainous and forested. Four major European geographic regions meet there: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, the Dinarides, the Pannonian Plain and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a>. The highest peak, Triglav, at 2,864 metres, appears on the national coat of arms and flag. The Adriatic coastline stretches approximately 47 kilometres, the southwest has a limestone Karst Plateau of underground rivers, gorges and caves, and the landscape of the northeast is essentially flat. The name means land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Evidence of habitation dates to around 250,000 years ago. A pierced cave bear bone from Divje Babe cave near Cerkno, dated to 43100 ± 700 years before present, is considered a kind of flute and possibly the oldest musical instrument discovered. Pile dwellings over 4,500 years old in the Ljubljana Marsh are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and the Ljubljana Marshes Wooden Wheel is the world's oldest wooden wheel. In Roman times the area was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> et Histria and the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and Noricum, with posts at Emona (Ljubljana), Poetovio (Ptuj) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celje">Celeia</a> (Celje). In 394 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-frigidus">Battle of the Frigidus</a> took place in the Vipava Valley, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> defeated the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugenius">Eugenius</a>.</p><p>Slavic tribes migrated to the Alpine area in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. From 623 or 624 King Samo united the Alpine and Western Slavs against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, and after his death around 658 the ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> formed Carantania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. Charlemagne decisively defeated the Avars in 803. The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, and Slovenes was formed in December 1918 and became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1929. Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> partitioned Slovenia in 1941.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Slovenia was established in 1945 as one of six republics of the Socialist Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and modern Slovenia emerged in June 1991, when it declared independence and became a sovereign state after the brief Ten-Day War. It is a parliamentary republic whose head of state is a president elected for five years, and legislative power rests mainly with the 90-member National Assembly. In 2007 it was the first new member state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> to adopt the euro. The population is about 2.1 million, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> make up 83.1% of the inhabitants. Four sites are on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage list, among them the Škocjan Caves and the Idrija Mercury mining site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Slovenia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The NDH covered most of present-day Croatia, all of Bosnia and Herzegovina, part of Serbia and a small part of <b>Slovenia</b>, while Italy and Hungary took Dalmatia, the islands, Međimurje and southern Baranja.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom united some twelve million people in 1921, rising to about sixteen million by 1941, from regions with very different pasts: former Habsburg <b>Slovenia</b>, Croatia, Vojvodina and Bosnia-Herzegovina, and the formerly Ottoman lands of Serbia, Montenegro, Kosovo and Macedonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The regions diverged: in 1965 <b>Slovenia</b>'s income per head was 177.3% of the national figure and Kosovo's 38.6%.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A holy place at Kobarid, in present-day <b>Slovenia</b>, was stamped out as late as 1331.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The fashion for decorated bronze buckets, situlae, spread north from about 600 to 400 BCE; the Vače situla from <b>Slovenia</b> is an example.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>The Counter-Reformation succeeded in drastically diminishing Protestantism in Lithuania, Poland, France and Italy and in the vast lands of the Habsburgs, among them Austria, southern Germany, Bohemia, the Spanish Netherlands, Croatia and <b>Slovenia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
